abstract:
ABSTRACT 

                    Atypical sensory processing is commonly observed in autism, particularly when integration across sensory modalities is required. The neural mechanisms underlying multisensory differences remain unclear. We recorded EEG while autistic children aged 8–13 (AU;
                     n 
                    = 40), siblings of autistic children (SIB;
                     n 
                    = 26), and non‐autistic controls (NA;
                     n 
                    = 36) performed a simple reaction‐time task to auditory (A), visual (V), and audiovisual (AV) stimuli. Analyses targeted event‐related potentials (ERPs), alpha‐band event‐related desynchronization (α‐ERD), and long‐range theta‐band functional connectivity. Groups did not differ across unisensory measures, indicating comparable unisensory processing. By contrast, multisensory integration (MSI;AV‐(A+V)) differed across groups: NA children showed significant ERP MSI over parieto‐central sites that was absent in AU and SIB; and α‐ERD MSI was significantly reduced in AU, with SIB showing an intermediate profile. Connectivity analyses revealed reduced AV fronto‐parieto‐occipital coupling in autistic children relative to non‐autistic children, suggesting weaker large‐scale coordination during multisensory processing. These results point to a multisensory‐specific deficit spanning early sensory encoding, posterior α‐ERD, and fronto‐posterior coupling and support disrupted multisensory influences on sensory processing due to reduced multisensory attentional orienting. SIB profiles suggest inherited liability for these neural phenotypes. These results help explain well‐documented behavioral MSI differences in autism by linking impaired early enhancement with attenuated top‐down control of sensory cortex.

Habitat use is strongly interlinked with population dynamics and plays an important role in population regulation. Animals frequently modify their habitat use behavior in response to major changes in population density. This is well studied in contexts wherein resources are limiting: for example, when changes in habitat use behavior reduce pressure from resource limitations. Situations wherein resource limitations are relaxed, such as upon recovery from severe declines, remain poorly studied. Here, we study how the spatial distribution of a nearly extirpated animal population changed during population recovery. We study a population of southern right whales (
                     Eubalaena australis 
                    ), a migratory species that was nearly extirpated by whaling. Using large-scale annual aerial survey data (1976–2023), we assess how spatial distribution patterns at nursing grounds changed as annual coastal abundances grew from few individuals to a few hundred. This includes assessment of the species’ characteristic site fidelity at a uniquely large spatiotemporal scale. We observed weaker fidelity to specific sites than generally assumed for Australian southern right whales and found strong indications that conspecific attraction influences the species’ coastal habitat selection. Distribution patterns initially displayed strong dispersal but became increasingly clustered coincident with substantial growth in annual whale abundances. This transition captures a rare example of a population crossing a behavioral threshold, possibly reflecting a switch in behavioral strategy or lack of conspecific cues. Our findings suggest that the behavioral processes governing habitat selection can shift with population density, driving substantial reorganization of populations’ spatial structure during substantial demographic change.

title:
Hemolymph pressure-mediated friction in the tarsus of the desert locust (
                     Schistocerca gregaria 
                    )

abstract:
The desert locust (
                     Schistocerca gregaria 
                    ) performs high-acceleration jumps within milliseconds, relying not only on powerful muscles and spring-loaded hindlegs, but also on precise tarsi-substrate interactions. Although the mechanics of jumping have been extensively studied, the mechanisms enabling rapid attachment and detachment remain poorly understood. Here, we characterize the frictional properties of hindleg tarsi under controlled low-speed contact conditions targeting selected loading features associated with jumping. We hypothesize that locusts coordinate tarsal cuticular structures with internal hemolymph pressure to modulate contact mechanics. Using high-speed videography, a suite of advanced microscopic techniques, biomechanical experiments, and biomimetic prototypes, we provide evidence that hemolymph pressure modulates tarsal stiffness and acts together with structural asymmetry to produce direction-dependent frictional responses. This interplay may contribute to adaptive friction tuning associated with jumping. Our findings advance the understanding of locust locomotor biomechanics and suggest design principles for robotic systems requiring rapid and controllable surface engagement.

Perceptual decision-making is well studied in mammals, but its operation in other vertebrates remains unclear. We show that carrion crows (
                     Corvus corone 
                    ) make deliberate decisions in a classic visual motion discrimination task with unprecedented speed, reporting choices in approximately 235 milliseconds on average, which is roughly twice as fast as has been observed in monkeys or humans and without sacrificing accuracy. Recordings from the pallial endbrain reveal that direction-selective neurons encode the upcoming choice as early as about 70 milliseconds after stimulus onset and about 170 milliseconds before the response, with ramping activity scaling with sensory evidence and converging at response onset. These findings highlight the extraordinary efficiency of avian decision-making circuitry, possibly shaped by evolutionary pressures for rapid visuomotor control.

Division of labor in social insects is enabled by coordinated changes in physiology, neural signaling, and gene regulation, yet it remains unclear to what extent behavioral maturation reflects incremental changes in individual genes and/or a reorganization of transcriptomic regulation at the system level. Here, we analyze the nurse-to-forager transition in worker honeybees (
                     Apis mellifera 
                    ) from a network perspective, treating the transcriptome as a gene–gene correlation system whose global organization reflects collective regulatory states. Using fat body gene expression data across five developmental time points that span an interval of behavioral transition, we construct signed gene–gene correlation networks and quantify their macro- and mesostate properties. We then descend to the level of the gene and construct microstate gene lists of interacting genes. Behavioral maturation of worker honeybees is accompanied by coordinated, nonmonotonic changes in global network structure rather than simple global shifts in interaction strength across the network. Early stages exhibit broadly positive, globally synchronized correlations, whereas later stages show increased modular segregation and the emergence of antagonistic gene–gene relationships. Despite this differentiation, networks remain fully connected with high global efficiency, consistent with a flexible small-world–like organization. Positive correlations primarily support global integration, while negative correlations act as localized, boundary-enforcing constraints. Negative correlations form a structured pattern of antagonism that is sparse and module-specific, occurring primarily between functional gene modules rather than within them. Network layouts and module analyses reveal two polarized, attractor-like regulatory states, a structure recapitulated in a vitellogenin-centered subnetwork and consistent with prior experimental perturbation studies.

Marine heatwaves are increasingly recognized for their capacity to generate ecological surprises, yet the pathways that propagate these unexpected responses are often poorly understood. Here, we document an unanticipated ecological response 7 y after a Bering Sea marine heatwave, in which formerly rare snow crab (
                     Chionoecetes opilio 
                    ) × Tanner crab (
                     C. bairdi 
                    ) hybrid abundance increased 13.4-fold, and the proportion of commercial-sized hybrid males increased 13.1-fold, relative to historical averages. Using causal modeling, we show that the hybrid crab surge was mediated by a heatwave-induced collapse of snow crab rather than direct thermal effects on hybrid survival or increased spatial overlap between mature snow and Tanner crab. Our results reveal an indirect cascading pathway through which an extreme climate event triggered unexpected ecological consequences and unforeseen challenges for fisheries management in the Bering Sea. The dramatic increase in abundance of harvestable hybrid crab, coupled with their exclusion from existing management frameworks, highlights the vulnerability of inflexible management systems to unanticipated ecosystem responses.

ABSTRACT 

                    Blood‐brain barrier (BBB) preserves brain homeostasis yet restricts therapeutics; in bacterial meningitis, antibiotics’ limited penetration impairs delivery to the parenchyma and cerebrospinal fluid, constraining efficacy and motivating strategies to overcome the barrier. To address this, we developed a chiral virus‐mimicking mesoporous silica nanostructure (CVSN) functionalized with L‐alanine for antibiotic delivery. It offers several distinct advantages: (1) nanoscale dimensions facilitating BBB transport; (2) virus‐like morphology enhancing vascular accumulation and penetration, providing a foundation for passive targeting; (3) chiral surface modification conferring active recognition capacity for more efficient biological targeting; and (4) a mesoporous architecture that ensures high drug‐loading efficiency and controlled release of drugs. Experimentally, it achieves efficient BBB penetration into the brain parenchyma and shows targeted delivery into microglia and astrocytes. Simulating the loading of cargoes by viruses, it ultimately improves the bioavailability and brain parenchyma and cerebrospinal fluid contents of various antibiotics with distinct physicochemical properties, antibacterial spectrum, and BBB penetration capacity, including cefoperazone, norfloxacin, levofloxacin and imipenem. It helps to treat meningitis induced by both Gram‐negative bacteria (e.g.,
                     Escherichia coli 
                    ) and Gram‐positive bacteria (e.g.,
                     Staphylococcus aureus 
                    ) and provides a promising avenue to overcome the limitations imposed by the BBB for CNS infections antibiotic therapy in clinic.

title:
Breaking the tetrahedral constraint of tetracoordination: Square-planar [ICl
                     4 
                    ] enables unprecedented birefringence in [C(NH
                     2 
                    )
                     3 
                    ][ICl
                     4 
                    ]

abstract:
Birefringent crystals are indispensable for optoelectronic applications, yet commercially available crystals still suffer from the drawback of insufficient birefringence. Meanwhile, tetracoordination units—traditionally confined to a tetrahedral geometry—have long been sidelined from mainstream high-performance birefringent materials due to their intrinsic nearly negligible polarizability anisotropy. Herein, we break this tetrahedral constraint—demonstrating tetracoordinate square-planar [ICl
                     4 
                    ] anions as a revolutionary platform for unprecedented birefringence. To achieve this, we report an improved synthesis method that avoids the use of toxic Cl
                     2 
                    gas, enabling the preparation of [C(NH
                     2 
                    )
                     3 
                    ][ICl
                     4 
                    ] featuring square-planar [ICl
                     4 
                    ] units. This compound displays a birefringence of 0.975@550 nm, setting a new record that surpasses all existing compounds containing tetracoordinate structural units. The giant birefringence of this material is attributed to the strong polarization anisotropy induced by the unique square-planar geometry of the [ICl
                     4 
                    ] tetracoordinate units. This research provides theoretical guidance for designing high-performance polar materials based on tetracoordinate units in giant birefringent materials and promotes applications in related optoelectronic device.

ABSTRACT 

                    Energy harvesting from low‐acceleration, broadband vehicular vibrations remains challenging due to low‐amplitude and random frequency nature of such excitations, often poorly matched to conventional narrowband spring‐mass designs. Herein, a hybrid spring‐coupled broadband vehicle vibration energy harvesting(VEH) and triboelectric vibration sensing(TVS) module is proposed that exploits coupled mechanical resonance and frequency disparity between spring elements to achieve vibration amplification under low‐amplitude and wide‐frequency excitations. VEH combines the motion dynamics of high stiffness 3D printed multiturn wave spring and low‐stiffness metallic disc spring, enabling simultaneous vibration of coil and magnet for enhanced mechanoelectrical conversion over braoder frequency (1–30 Hz). Simulations and experiments confirmed that the dual‐spring coupling yields superior mechanoelectrical conversion (above 50%) under 0.5 g with peak and average powers of 260 mW(5.72 mW/cm
                     3 
                    ) and 88 mW(1.89 mW/cm
                     3 
                    ). Furthermore, lightweight spiral spring‐integration with PAN‐CuSCN composite nanofiber provides self‐powered vibration sensing through its pronounced tribonegative response as compared to the pristine nanofiber, enabling accurate road classification(>95%) and speed classification (84.1%) with minimum detection upto 0.075g. This dual‐functional module combining broadband energy harvesting, self‐powered vibration sensing with miniaturized PCB, integrated sensors and BLE allows continuous operation in realistic vehicular vibration environments, establishing a reliable framework for self‐sustainable vehicular environmental monitoring systems.

Hydrogen-methane blends are gaining attention as transitional fuels, but their safe storage and controlled release remain a challenge. Advancing this approach necessitates the development of compounds and materials capable of solid-state storage of mixed fuel gases. Molecular precursors capable of delivering both gases from a single source are not known. In this context, we report a low–molecular weight magnesium hydridomethylzincate, Mg[(CH
                     3 
                    )ZnH
                     3 
                    ], a crystalline, hydride-rich organometallic compound that does not rely on bulky supporting ligands. Under solvent-free, anaerobic conditions, it releases hydrogen and methane at temperatures as low as 80°C via reductive elimination, leaving behind elemental magnesium and zinc. The ability to liberate mixed fuel gases at relatively low temperatures underscores the potential of well-defined organometallic compounds as single-source precursors for these gases.

Straight faults are considered prone to large supershear ruptures and seismic gaps to likely host future earthquakes. Both predictions may have been fulfilled in the magnitude (
                     M 
                    ) 7.7 Mandalay earthquake of 28 March 2025 on the Sagaing Fault. Exceptional video footage of the fault rupture, high-rate Global Navigation Satellite System, and seismological and remote sensing data reveal a supershear slip pulse that formed early and ruptured a well-identified seismic gap but propagated well beyond it, resulting in an exceptional rupture length of 510 kilometers. By contrast, the slip pulse observed in the video footage has subshear characteristics. These observations can be reconciled by a preferred conceptual interpretation invoking depth-dependent weakening properties, stress heterogeneity, and possible dynamic triggering. These properties allow for energetic ruptures that stay confined to the depth range locked in the interseismic period but are not confined to seismic gaps along strike.

title:
Correction to “High‐Resolution Genomic Resources for Trait Mapping and Precision Breeding for Adzuki Bean (
                     Vigna angularis 
